# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: `EventProcessor`

## Key Problems in the Original Code

| Issue | Why it breaks on .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — removed in .NET 5+ |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Still exists but is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006` in .NET 8); unreliable for cancellation |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` + `Queue` | Works, but `Channel<T>` replaces all three cleanly |
| Race condition | Multiple `Enqueue` calls can signal before `Dequeue` runs, losing items |
| No cancellation token | No cooperative shutdown mechanism |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _processingTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        // Task.Run replaces manual Thread creation
        _processingTask = Task.Run(RunLoopAsync);
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            // ReadAllAsync blocks cooperatively until items arrive or cancellation
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Expected on shutdown — no action needed
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        // TryWrite on unbounded channel always succeeds (unless completed)
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Processor has been stopped.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        // 1. Signal no more writes — ReadAllAsync will drain remaining items
        _channel.Writer.Complete();

        // 2. Wait for drain, then cancel if it takes too long
        if (_processingTask is not null)
        {
            using var timeoutCts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            try
            {
                await _processingTask.WaitAsync(timeoutCts.Token);
            }
            catch (TimeoutException)
            {
                // Drain took too long — force cancel
                await _cts.CancelAsync();
                await _processingTask; // let it finish the catch block
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) => Console.WriteLine($"Processing: {item}");
}
```

## What Changed and Why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` | `Task.Run` + `Channel<T>` | Channel handles signaling, queuing, and thread-safety internally |
| `lock` + `Queue<T>` | `Channel<T>` | Channels are concurrent by design — no manual locking |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` | Cooperative cancellation is the only supported pattern |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` | Signals the reader to drain and exit naturally |
| Fire-and-forget `Thread` | `Task` with `await` | Enables proper error propagation and shutdown |

## Pitfalls to Watch For

1. **`Thread.Abort` is not negotiable** — it throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. There is no workaround; you *must* switch to `CancellationToken`.

2. **Race condition in the original** — two rapid `Enqueue` calls could signal `Set()` before the loop calls `Reset()`, causing only one `Dequeue` while two items exist. `Channel<T>` eliminates this entirely.

3. **Don't use `Thread.Interrupt()` as a substitute** — it's obsolete and only wakes `Thread.Sleep`/`Join`/`WaitOne`; it doesn't interrupt `await` or channel reads.

4. **Graceful vs. forced shutdown** — the migrated code first drains remaining items (`Writer.Complete()`), then cancels only if the timeout expires. This avoids data loss.

5. **If `Handle()` is async**, change the signature to `async Task HandleAsync(string item)` and `await` it inside the loop — unlike the thread-based version, `async` composes naturally.
